---
import DeepDiveLayout from '../../layouts/DeepDiveLayout.astro';
---

<DeepDiveLayout
  title="Deep Dive into the Firing Sequence"
  pillar="Sequence"
  chapterNumber="03"
  nextTitle="Barrel Path"
  nextHref="/learn/barrel-path"
>
  <section>
    <h2>Quick Recap</h2>

    <p>
      Once the load is complete, the body begins transferring force from the
      ground to the baseball.
    </p>

    <p>
      A quality firing sequence allows each segment of the body to contribute
      at the proper time.
    </p>

    <p>
      Efficient sequence creates speed with less effort.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Before We Begin</h2>

    <p>
      Every successful swing follows the same basic order.
    </p>

    <p>
      The body begins transferring force from the ground, through the lower
      half, into the torso, through the hands, and finally into the barrel.
    </p>

    <p>
      The sequence may happen in only a fraction of a second, but the order
      remains remarkably consistent.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every hitter develops an individual style.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      The sequence stays the same.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What We Want From the Sequence</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Force begins from the ground.</li>
      <li>The front side stops forward movement.</li>
      <li>The hips begin rotating.</li>
      <li>The torso follows the hips.</li>
      <li>The hands fire toward the contact position.</li>
      <li>The barrel follows naturally.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="callout">
      Every segment prepares the next.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Chain Reaction</h2>

    <p>
      The firing sequence is not a collection of independent movements.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each segment creates the conditions for the next segment to accelerate.
    </p>

    <p>
      When one segment fires too early or too late, every segment that follows
      must compensate.
    </p>

    <h3>The Front Side</h3>

    <p>
      Heel plant completes the load.
    </p>

    <p>
      The front leg accepts and resists the body's forward movement, allowing
      force to begin transferring upward.
    </p>

    <h3>The Hips</h3>

    <p>
      The hips begin rotating toward the contact position.
    </p>

    <p>
      The axis of rotation will naturally vary depending on pitch location.
    </p>

    <h3>The Torso</h3>

    <p>
      As the hips rotate, tension develops through the torso.
    </p>

    <p>
      The torso then accelerates toward contact.
    </p>

    <h3>The Hands</h3>

    <p>
      Once the decision to swing has been made, the hands fire directly toward
      the contact position.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      The hands fire to the contact position. The barrel follows.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Drills</h2>

    <div class="drill-grid">

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Pause at Heel Plant</h3>
        <p>
          Feel where the load ends and the firing sequence begins.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Separation Holds</h3>
        <p>
          Learn to create and maintain hip-to-torso separation before firing.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Medicine Ball Rotations</h3>
        <p>
          Develop the feeling of transferring force from the ground through the
          torso.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Ground-Up Swings</h3>
        <p>
          Focus on allowing the sequence to begin from the lower half rather
          than the hands.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Beginning with the hands.</li>
      <li>Hips spinning without transferring force.</li>
      <li>Landing and firing simultaneously.</li>
      <li>Front side continuing forward after heel plant.</li>
      <li>Losing separation too early.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="callout">
      Every early movement forces a later compensation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Self Evaluation</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Does force begin from the ground?</li>
      <li>Does my front side stop my forward movement?</li>
      <li>Do my hips begin before my torso?</li>
      <li>Do my hands fire to the contact position?</li>
      <li>Does the barrel arrive naturally?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</DeepDiveLayout>